178
00:14:03,879 --> 00:14:05,560
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's where you measure your actual faith.

179
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:06,861
[SPEAKER_05]: That's... we said that before.

180
00:14:06,881 --> 00:14:17,207
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what, like, somebody... like an actual sotāpāna is not an anāgāmī yet, because he might be lacking faith within, you know, within his great state of faith and confidence.

181
00:14:17,728 --> 00:14:20,129
[SPEAKER_05]: On some specific aspects, he might not have enough faith.

182
00:14:20,670 --> 00:14:20,990
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

183
00:14:21,070 --> 00:14:25,653
[SPEAKER_05]: To stick to certain behaviors, they would be taking him away from sensuality further.

184
00:14:26,842 --> 00:14:29,163
[SPEAKER_05]: So, he would review that.

185
00:14:29,223 --> 00:14:35,466
[SPEAKER_05]: He could review that and realize, oh, I lack faith in those crucial little moments of giving in, here and there.

186
00:14:36,186 --> 00:14:44,550
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what if I don't give in and build up my faith through remembering, through recollecting the danger, the peril, the dissatisfaction?

187
00:14:46,531 --> 00:14:50,272
[SPEAKER_05]: It goes every... against everything that I already work for, pretty much, at that point.

188
00:14:50,752 --> 00:14:56,535
[SPEAKER_05]: So, recollect that, ponder on that frequently, as the Buddha would say, and the mind will lean in that direction.

189
00:14:57,528 --> 00:15:05,592
[SPEAKER_05]: But see, people don't ponder on things frequently at all, with that kind of intent, right intent behind it.

190
00:15:06,332 --> 00:15:26,582
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, they might ponder on something, and then adopt the view on account of it, and that's it, my work's done, and then now give me a calming technique, a practice, sensation watching, a bit of this, a bit of external duty, I just do this because everybody else does, and I shall not be reflecting or pondering or revisiting the stuff again, because that is unpleasant.

191
00:15:28,176 --> 00:15:30,598
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's like, no, the practice is frequently recollect.

192
00:15:31,178 --> 00:15:40,484
[SPEAKER_05]: Again, not mechanically go through the words and mantras, but revise, recollect, bring up... Ideas.

193
00:15:40,784 --> 00:15:41,905
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

194
00:15:41,925 --> 00:15:49,070
[SPEAKER_05]: Ideas, contexts, information that you learned before, until the mind actually starts to settle in there.

195
00:15:50,450 --> 00:15:53,773
[SPEAKER_05]: And the more often you do that right type of recollection,

196
00:15:54,636 --> 00:15:56,799
[SPEAKER_05]: The calmer the mind would be, the less disturbed.

197
00:15:56,879 --> 00:16:12,880
[SPEAKER_05]: And same when the Buddha says in the suttas, the householder who has the right view, his samādhi, his pleasant abiding, like jhāna for monks, is when he frequently recollects the Buddha, the dhamma, the saṅgha, the unbroken virtue.

198
00:16:14,521 --> 00:16:27,249
[SPEAKER_05]: Again, not as a blind recital to a golden shrine, but actually recollects the value, the meaning, the point, his involvement, makes it alive through recollection and diligent mental effort.

199
00:16:28,049 --> 00:16:30,771
[SPEAKER_05]: And there and then, he has pleasant abiding there and now.

200
00:16:31,672 --> 00:16:32,492
[SPEAKER_05]: Nothing can touch him.

201
00:16:34,874 --> 00:16:35,274
[SPEAKER_02]: Sati.

202
00:16:35,874 --> 00:16:36,414
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

203
00:16:36,495 --> 00:16:36,835
[SPEAKER_05]: Sati.

204
00:16:36,875 --> 00:16:38,756
[SPEAKER_05]: You can't have too much of recollection.

205
00:16:43,340 --> 00:16:50,264
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if somebody lacks faith, it's because they haven't recollected enough, yeah, haven't dwelt on it long enough, haven't clarified it long enough.

206
00:16:51,365 --> 00:16:52,805
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's... that is the measure, actually.

207
00:16:52,865 --> 00:16:56,327
[SPEAKER_05]: Somebody can be full of faith, but has zero confidence.

208
00:16:56,828 --> 00:17:02,711
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, if you were to ask them, if you were to interrogate them and question about the practice, they'll be all over the place and there'll be absolutely no confidence there.

209
00:17:04,212 --> 00:17:05,753
[SPEAKER_05]: They still might be blinded by that faith.

210
00:17:05,793 --> 00:17:07,213
[SPEAKER_05]: They still might not accept what you have to say.

211
00:17:07,794 --> 00:17:11,656
[SPEAKER_05]: But their lack of confidence in their own words will become very apparent.

212
00:17:12,082 --> 00:17:16,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, that's nearly... the more you lack confidence, the more faith you need.

213
00:17:16,344 --> 00:17:18,385
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you need to compensate it.

214
00:17:18,525 --> 00:17:19,906
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't see the discrepancy.

215
00:17:19,986 --> 00:17:28,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe you have real faith in this teacher, but the teaching is discrepant, or there are these contradictions which you're not seeing.

216
00:17:28,810 --> 00:17:33,772
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, nobody doubts that that faith is real as such, but it's misguided.

217
00:17:34,152 --> 00:17:36,233
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not authentic.

218
00:17:36,533 --> 00:17:37,033
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

219
00:17:37,574 --> 00:17:38,134
[SPEAKER_04]: Misplaced.

220
00:17:42,619 --> 00:17:56,598
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you know, it's just to say, like, the... he has confidence in the instruction, then he applies himself, becomes a sotāpanna, and... sorry, he has faith in the instruction, becomes a sotāpanna, and then he develops confirmed confidence in the teaching.

221
00:17:56,918 --> 00:18:00,383
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, he saw it for himself, now his confidence has been confirmed.

222
00:18:02,448 --> 00:18:05,930
[SPEAKER_04]: I was gonna say that's then that confirmed faith.

223
00:18:06,010 --> 00:18:06,350
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

224
00:18:06,370 --> 00:18:11,313
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you could say he has confidence to a degree, but he hasn't confirmed it still thoroughly for himself.

225
00:18:11,753 --> 00:18:16,917
[SPEAKER_05]: And for a sotāpanna, he has confirmed confidence in the teaching that is right, because he actually is free from suffering.

226
00:18:17,537 --> 00:18:21,640
[SPEAKER_05]: So, there is no greater verification of the truthfulness and the right practice there.

227
00:18:23,899 --> 00:18:26,220
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you could say he has faith, that then gets confirmed.

228
00:18:26,841 --> 00:18:32,244
[SPEAKER_05]: But see, when you know now that this works, you don't... there is no belief involved anymore.

229
00:18:32,505 --> 00:18:32,985
[SPEAKER_05]: Now you know.

230
00:18:33,225 --> 00:18:37,008
[SPEAKER_05]: You have confirmed it, that it works, so you don't need to believe it and hope that it works.

231
00:18:37,788 --> 00:18:38,168
[SPEAKER_05]: It works.

232
00:18:39,609 --> 00:18:46,154
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, like the sutta of the hen having to sit on its eggs for chicken to lay out, to come out safely.

233
00:18:46,575 --> 00:18:53,880
[SPEAKER_05]: You could say, first time, say, first time hen doesn't necessarily know that if she lays on the eggs, the chicks would come out safely.

234
00:18:55,321 --> 00:18:58,784
[SPEAKER_05]: He has some confidence and some faith that that's how it works.

235
00:18:59,997 --> 00:19:01,697
[SPEAKER_05]: And then it does... it does the work.

236
00:19:01,877 --> 00:19:04,118
[SPEAKER_05]: It sits on the eggs, and the chicks come out safely.

237
00:19:04,638 --> 00:19:09,219
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, for the next time, she won't have to question it again, because she has confirmed that that's what it works.

238
00:19:09,759 --> 00:19:16,080
[SPEAKER_05]: So, in the beginning, for the first time, she might have doubt... had a lot of doubt of, oh, should I get up now?

239
00:19:16,340 --> 00:19:17,041
[SPEAKER_05]: Should I stay?

240
00:19:17,161 --> 00:19:18,141
[SPEAKER_05]: How long should I stay?

241
00:19:18,201 --> 00:19:18,841
[SPEAKER_05]: Should I leave?

242
00:19:18,881 --> 00:19:20,121
[SPEAKER_05]: How quickly I have to return?

243
00:19:20,501 --> 00:19:21,381
[SPEAKER_05]: Maybe this won't work.

244
00:19:21,421 --> 00:19:23,282
[SPEAKER_05]: Maybe I need to leave them alone for them to hatch.

245
00:19:23,362 --> 00:19:25,982
[SPEAKER_05]: Maybe... All of these thoughts would have been passing through its head.

246
00:19:26,623 --> 00:19:28,083
[SPEAKER_05]: But if it actually sat on its eggs

247
00:19:29,038 --> 00:19:36,467
[SPEAKER_05]: So, next time, she won't have the same doubts again, because all she needs to do is just not give in to those doubts, because she knows that what she's doing is already right.

248
00:19:49,738 --> 00:19:58,765
[SPEAKER_02]: So, she doesn't rely on any circumstance for her confidence, that her confidence is independent of any... At that point, afterwards, yes, exactly, exactly, exactly.

249
00:19:58,805 --> 00:20:00,206
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, nothing can come in between that.

250
00:20:00,366 --> 00:20:10,774
[SPEAKER_05]: That's exactly what it was described in the suttas by that Sotāpanna who said, well, even if the whole world says one thing and the Buddha said the other, the opposite, like, the Buddha's right.

251
00:20:11,255 --> 00:20:13,917
[SPEAKER_05]: Not because he's a fundamentalist, blinded lunatic.

252
00:20:14,637 --> 00:20:17,320
[SPEAKER_05]: No, it's because he actually confirmed that thing for himself.

253
00:20:18,314 --> 00:20:19,615
[SPEAKER_05]: So, nobody can come in between that.

254
00:20:20,096 --> 00:20:23,239
[SPEAKER_05]: If people say the opposite, it's because they haven't seen it for themselves.

255
00:20:24,440 --> 00:20:26,502
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's directly visible here and now.

256
00:20:28,544 --> 00:20:35,871
[SPEAKER_05]: And that is, and that should be the criteria, by the way, even for somebody before they become a sotāpanna and start practicing and so on.

257
00:20:36,612 --> 00:20:41,177
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, the goals, the results should be visible here and now.

258
00:20:43,317 --> 00:20:43,557
[SPEAKER_05]: You know.

259
00:20:43,977 --> 00:20:44,898
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay.

260
00:20:44,918 --> 00:20:45,818
[SPEAKER_05]: It won't happen overnight.

261
00:20:45,898 --> 00:20:46,818
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not an instant thing.

262
00:20:46,978 --> 00:20:47,999
[SPEAKER_05]: You have to sit on the eggs.

263
00:20:48,759 --> 00:20:53,041
[SPEAKER_05]: But, okay, but, you know, I've been doing it for 20 years now.

264
00:20:53,921 --> 00:20:54,741
[SPEAKER_05]: Isn't that a bit long?

265
00:20:57,122 --> 00:20:57,883
[SPEAKER_05]: Where is the right view?

266
00:20:58,763 --> 00:21:01,384
[SPEAKER_05]: I've been following this teaching or that teaching for how long?

267
00:21:01,864 --> 00:21:02,264
[SPEAKER_05]: Two days?

268
00:21:02,324 --> 00:21:02,564
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay.

269
00:21:02,684 --> 00:21:03,745
[SPEAKER_05]: Maybe follow a bit longer.

270
00:21:04,245 --> 00:21:04,705
[SPEAKER_05]: Two years?

271
00:21:04,865 --> 00:21:05,165
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay.

272
00:21:05,245 --> 00:21:05,525
[SPEAKER_05]: You know.

273
00:21:05,765 --> 00:21:07,666
[SPEAKER_05]: Is there any anything to show for?

274
00:21:07,926 --> 00:21:08,426
[SPEAKER_05]: Five years?

275
00:21:08,507 --> 00:21:08,907
[SPEAKER_05]: Ten years?

276
00:21:08,987 --> 00:21:09,227
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay.

277
00:21:09,267 --> 00:21:09,727
[SPEAKER_05]: Come on now.

278
00:21:12,713 --> 00:21:21,996
[SPEAKER_05]: But not like... just dismiss that part of results and still just convince yourself that you're doing it right.

279
00:21:22,496 --> 00:21:22,656
[SPEAKER_05]: No?

280
00:21:23,277 --> 00:21:24,217
[SPEAKER_05]: It should be happening.

281
00:21:26,378 --> 00:21:39,942
[SPEAKER_05]: So, yes, there is a such thing to be a bit impatient, but then there is a such thing to, like, completely disregard the fact that you will not know whether you're doing it rightly until you confirmed it, that it is the right thing to do.

282
00:21:40,002 --> 00:21:42,383
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, until you know for yourself, until you're a sotāpanna.

283
00:21:50,023 --> 00:21:53,764
[SPEAKER_01]: It comes down to not really disregard... not disregarding... Not disregarding the goal.

284
00:21:53,904 --> 00:21:54,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Your own aim.

285
00:21:54,724 --> 00:21:55,085
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

286
00:21:55,245 --> 00:21:56,045
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my aim?

287
00:21:56,465 --> 00:22:09,069
[SPEAKER_05]: When I have an aim, I do what it takes to get... Now, that aim, obviously, as I said, as you discern more, will become more refined, let's say, let's put it that way, than what it used to be.

288
00:22:09,589 --> 00:22:12,870
[SPEAKER_05]: But fundamentally, you need to keep it in the play.

289
00:22:13,090 --> 00:22:14,871
[SPEAKER_05]: You need to keep it there as a relevant thing.

290
00:22:15,451 --> 00:22:17,812
[SPEAKER_05]: Because you are doing this to be free from suffering.

291
00:22:19,055 --> 00:22:20,016
[SPEAKER_05]: Are you free from suffering?

292
00:22:20,156 --> 00:22:24,738
[SPEAKER_05]: Do you know the way out of suffering for yourself, independent of any circumstances, exactly?

293
00:22:25,419 --> 00:22:26,680
[SPEAKER_05]: Can something come in between it?

294
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:31,042
[SPEAKER_05]: Can something cause doubt that would completely change the course of your effort?

295
00:22:33,104 --> 00:22:39,647
[SPEAKER_05]: Or do you know for yourself, beyond doubt, cannot be shaken, you know exactly, what suffering is and what escape from it is?

296
00:22:39,968 --> 00:22:41,789
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly, there's nothing that can come in between.

297
00:22:41,889 --> 00:22:43,330
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like the most direct thing you see.

298
00:22:44,130 --> 00:22:45,691
[SPEAKER_05]: And feel, obviously, by that point.

299
00:22:47,592 --> 00:22:48,713
[SPEAKER_04]: And you can kind of apply that

300
00:22:53,948 --> 00:23:04,928
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, way of thinking, even if you don't know for yourself, you can think, well, is my current understanding along the lines that could not be

301
00:23:06,605 --> 00:23:08,807
[SPEAKER_04]: Turned upside down by something or whatever.

302
00:23:08,827 --> 00:23:09,387
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

303
00:23:10,027 --> 00:23:16,452
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, well, kind of find out you can't go into, like, particulars and turn them around to fit.

304
00:23:16,692 --> 00:23:17,833
[SPEAKER_04]: Without changing the whole thing.

305
00:23:17,853 --> 00:23:18,473
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

306
00:23:18,614 --> 00:23:29,241
[SPEAKER_04]: But kind of to, well, to find where your understanding or misunderstanding does not have that nature of being unable to be, well, yeah, mistaken or turned upside down.

307
00:23:29,261 --> 00:23:30,962
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, needs to be, needs to be sound.

308
00:23:31,282 --> 00:23:32,984
[SPEAKER_05]: Needs to be free from contradiction.

309
00:23:34,024 --> 00:23:34,765
[SPEAKER_05]: Free from, eh...

310
00:23:35,478 --> 00:23:50,170
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, you might be emotionally invested in it, and which is why it would be unpleasant to question it, and you're emotionally invested because, you know, freedom from suffering is taken personally, it's important, so that's fine, that's understandable, but that should not prevent you from questioning it.

311
00:23:52,071 --> 00:23:52,952
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you need to know

312
00:23:54,072 --> 00:24:01,714
[SPEAKER_05]: That it is a sound thing, that can hold the water, as it said, and, eh... so... and that exactly, it cannot be upturned by... by the opposing view.

313
00:24:02,274 --> 00:24:08,196
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, you would see opposing view, where the lack... where it lacks, but it doesn't add up, because you thought your view through.

314
00:24:08,476 --> 00:24:20,379
[SPEAKER_05]: You didn't just accept it, took it on, and now, you know, a disagreement comes, or... or... or something that says, oh, well, no, that's wrong, and you... you just... you just go in a defense mode without recognizing, wait, but do I see where this applies?

315
00:24:20,399 --> 00:24:20,939
[SPEAKER_05]: Maybe it's right

316
00:24:22,928 --> 00:24:23,649
[SPEAKER_05]: What if it is right?

317
00:24:23,689 --> 00:24:24,829
[SPEAKER_05]: Do I know that it isn't right?

318
00:24:24,849 --> 00:24:25,730
[SPEAKER_05]: Do I know that I'm right?

319
00:24:26,871 --> 00:24:30,654
[SPEAKER_05]: Or do I just desperately need to believe that I'm right and have to shut everything else out?

320
00:24:32,876 --> 00:24:45,145
[SPEAKER_05]: Even if you're not, if you don't know for yourself yet, you can certainly not find fault or any contradiction in the value of sense restraint and saying no to sensuality.

321
00:24:45,585 --> 00:24:46,726
[SPEAKER_04]: You'll find a lot of pain in it.

322
00:24:47,835 --> 00:24:56,121
[SPEAKER_05]: But that's not to say that it's not a logically sound conclusion, that it's better to be withdrawn from desires and not act out of them, and not scratching the itch.

323
00:24:57,081 --> 00:24:57,522
[SPEAKER_05]: You know that.

324
00:24:57,602 --> 00:24:58,883
[SPEAKER_05]: Colloquially, you already know that.

325
00:24:58,963 --> 00:24:59,723
[SPEAKER_05]: Everybody knows that.

326
00:25:00,263 --> 00:25:07,528
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just they don't have enough reasons, because they haven't recollected sufficiently enough of the dangers, the context, the benefits.

327
00:25:08,249 --> 00:25:09,009
[SPEAKER_05]: So, they lack faith.

328
00:25:09,610 --> 00:25:13,052
[SPEAKER_05]: They lack faith in sticking to it.

329
00:25:13,092 --> 00:25:14,193
[SPEAKER_03]: This is that sutta I was mentioning?

330
00:25:14,473 --> 00:25:14,693
[SPEAKER_05]: Uh-huh.

331
00:25:18,503 --> 00:25:22,043
[SPEAKER_03]: This is the layman speaking to Venerable Ananda.

332
00:25:22,203 --> 00:25:29,945
[SPEAKER_03]: It says, Bhante Ananda, we laymen enjoy essential pleasures, take delight in essential pleasures and rejoice in essential pleasures.

333
00:25:29,965 --> 00:25:33,786
[SPEAKER_03]: Renunciation seems like a precipice to us.

334
00:25:33,846 --> 00:25:36,527
[SPEAKER_03]: I have heard that in this dhamma and discipline there are very young

335
00:25:37,647 --> 00:25:42,534
[SPEAKER_03]: Bhikkhus who, you know, go forth and delight in that renunciation.

336
00:25:43,476 --> 00:25:51,627
[SPEAKER_03]: But how come my mind doesn't delight in it, even though I know, in theory, all this, the teaching already?

337
00:25:53,449 --> 00:26:16,035
[SPEAKER_03]: So, anyway, there's a bit of a further discussion, but the Buddha says, before my enlightenment, while I was just a Bodhisatta, it occurred to me, good is renunciation, good is solitude, yet my mind did not launch out upon renunciation and become calm, settled and liberated in it, though I saw it as peaceful.

338
00:26:17,075 --> 00:26:20,796
[SPEAKER_03]: It occurred to me, why is it that my mind does not launch out upon renunciation,

339
00:26:22,153 --> 00:26:27,496
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, it occurred to me, I have not seen the danger in sensual pleasures and I have not cultivated that insight.

340
00:26:28,216 --> 00:26:30,277
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's even before he was enlightened.

341
00:26:30,497 --> 00:26:30,637
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

342
00:26:30,857 --> 00:26:31,858
[SPEAKER_03]: I have not thought about it.

343
00:26:33,779 --> 00:26:37,641
[SPEAKER_03]: I have not achieved the benefit in renunciation and have not pursued it.

344
00:26:38,501 --> 00:26:41,443
[SPEAKER_03]: Therefore, my mind does not launch out upon renunciation.

345
00:26:43,144 --> 00:26:43,404
[SPEAKER_03]: And then,

346
00:26:48,495 --> 00:27:00,765
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, later he says, if having seen the danger in sensual pleasures, I would cultivate that insight, and if having achieved the benefit in renunciation, I would pursue it, since it is peaceful.

347
00:27:00,965 --> 00:27:04,848
[SPEAKER_03]: Sometime later, having seen the danger in sensual pleasures, I cultivated that insight.

348
00:27:05,228 --> 00:27:07,530
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, cultivate the insight.

349
00:27:07,690 --> 00:27:09,292
[SPEAKER_05]: Recollect it, reflect on it.

350
00:27:11,233 --> 00:27:14,556
[SPEAKER_05]: You get a degree of understanding, then cultivate that understanding further.

351
00:27:14,836 --> 00:27:16,718
[SPEAKER_05]: So that's your meditation, that's your practice.

352
00:27:17,935 --> 00:27:28,521
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not like mindlessly sitting and switching the thoughts off, and experiencing pleasant bodily sensations, and then waking up from it, and then not knowing how to deal with stuff when it starts to pressuring you, because you haven't dealt with anything.

353
00:27:28,541 --> 00:27:31,763
[SPEAKER_05]: You're just shutting yourself down, because you don't want to deal with stuff.

354
00:27:31,803 --> 00:27:32,583
[SPEAKER_05]: That's pretty much it.

355
00:27:33,264 --> 00:27:35,285
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's completely the opposite of what the practice is.

356
00:27:37,300 --> 00:28:00,819
[SPEAKER_05]: So reflect, recollect, sit down to meditate, quote-unquote, and by that I mean sit down and spend an hour, dedicated hour of your time, in like a motionless state of recollecting and reflecting and cultivating the insights of the danger, the peril, and when you get off your cushion, the danger, the traps, the baits will be all around you.

357
00:28:01,344 --> 00:28:15,757
[SPEAKER_05]: And you will not go towards them, and you will maintain the context of the dissatisfaction, and the precipice, if you do, and all of that, and learn how to live among the bait, without touching the bait, like the deer herd sutta.

358
00:28:15,777 --> 00:28:16,638
[SPEAKER_05]: You need to learn that.

359
00:28:17,079 --> 00:28:21,302
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not magically going to be revealed and highlighted little markers around the bait.

360
00:28:21,363 --> 00:28:21,483
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

361
00:28:21,523 --> 00:28:25,887
[SPEAKER_05]: You need to discern that, and maintain that, and then you become comfortable within it.

362
00:28:26,067 --> 00:28:27,068
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, obviously,

363
00:28:28,471 --> 00:28:34,176
[SPEAKER_05]: Having not been trapped for a long time, you're going to develop even more confidence in it, because you are actually free.

364
00:28:35,297 --> 00:28:38,921
[SPEAKER_05]: You can run in between the bait and you will not get caught.

365
00:28:39,241 --> 00:28:40,342
[SPEAKER_05]: That's how confident you'll be.

366
00:28:42,204 --> 00:28:44,326
[SPEAKER_05]: You won't need to shelter yourself in that sense.

367
00:28:45,186 --> 00:28:46,628
[SPEAKER_05]: But that need... that takes work.

368
00:28:46,868 --> 00:28:54,555
[SPEAKER_05]: Diligence, recollection, reflection, maintenance of the context and enduring of the pressure of acting out that you don't... don't give in to.

369
00:28:54,953 --> 00:28:56,834
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, and the sutta goes on.

370
00:28:57,174 --> 00:28:58,675
[SPEAKER_03]: You see the danger in that sensuality.

371
00:28:58,695 --> 00:28:59,856
[SPEAKER_03]: You cultivate that insight.

372
00:29:00,957 --> 00:29:04,499
[SPEAKER_03]: And the further away, you obviously see something as dangerous.

373
00:29:04,519 --> 00:29:07,441
[SPEAKER_03]: You're going to be moving away from it, dispassionate, not engaging.

374
00:29:08,282 --> 00:29:15,146
[SPEAKER_03]: And that results in the first jhāna, the peace of renunciation.

375
00:29:15,566 --> 00:29:16,607
[SPEAKER_03]: So, he's done nothing else.

376
00:29:17,305 --> 00:29:23,410
[SPEAKER_04]: Except reflect... Where the baits are, where the traps are, and withdrawing himself from the traps.

377
00:29:23,470 --> 00:29:26,092
[SPEAKER_05]: There is no... Well, I don't know what do I say, what should I say no to.

378
00:29:26,132 --> 00:29:27,993
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, because you haven't reflected on things long enough.

379
00:29:28,073 --> 00:29:29,615
[SPEAKER_05]: If you do, it will become clearer.

380
00:29:30,015 --> 00:29:36,420
[SPEAKER_05]: And there will be layers and layers that you can peel, until you specifically, directly recognize the roots of sensual desire.

381
00:29:36,520 --> 00:29:39,843
[SPEAKER_05]: In the beginning, yeah, you maybe have to say no to too many things.

382
00:29:40,503 --> 00:29:41,464
[SPEAKER_05]: Unnecessarily so.

383
00:29:41,584 --> 00:29:42,965
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it will cause you a bit more pain.

384
00:29:43,686 --> 00:29:45,167
[SPEAKER_05]: But if you make the effort to reflect,

385
00:29:46,110 --> 00:29:46,630
[SPEAKER_05]: Recollect.

386
00:29:46,911 --> 00:29:48,151
[SPEAKER_05]: Cultivate those insights.

387
00:29:48,952 --> 00:29:50,233
[SPEAKER_05]: It will crystallize more and more.

388
00:29:50,533 --> 00:29:52,374
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, you realize, no, I can relax, actually.

389
00:29:52,394 --> 00:29:53,155
[SPEAKER_05]: This is not dangerous.

390
00:29:53,175 --> 00:29:53,835
[SPEAKER_05]: That's not dangerous.

391
00:29:53,855 --> 00:29:55,016
[SPEAKER_05]: This is not rooted in sensuality.

392
00:29:55,496 --> 00:30:01,160
[SPEAKER_05]: But, measure for that is the internal knowledge, not the external criteria or the word of another.

393
00:30:03,061 --> 00:30:06,384
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, obviously, there are certain obvious things that are always rooted in sensuality.

394
00:30:06,464 --> 00:30:10,126
[SPEAKER_05]: So, things basically, eh... against the precepts.

395
00:30:10,847 --> 00:30:15,350
[SPEAKER_05]: And I mean, like, say, five precepts with the third precept being celibacy.

396
00:30:16,445 --> 00:30:19,508
[SPEAKER_05]: That's kind of, you know, intoxication, lying, stealing and all of that.

397
00:30:20,228 --> 00:30:25,132
[SPEAKER_05]: And sensual desire, distraction through your senses and sense objects, yeah.

398
00:30:25,312 --> 00:30:29,115
[SPEAKER_05]: So you need to say no to that, always, you know, like you can't go wrong there.

399
00:30:30,336 --> 00:30:34,500
[SPEAKER_05]: Because more often than not, your intention will always be the root in sensuality when it comes to those things.

400
00:30:35,381 --> 00:30:36,982
[SPEAKER_05]: But the cultivation needs to happen.

401
00:30:37,863 --> 00:30:40,425
[SPEAKER_05]: Often, frequently reflecting...

402
00:30:40,485 --> 00:30:41,966
[SPEAKER_03]: But just saying no to things...

403
00:30:43,192 --> 00:30:44,493
[SPEAKER_00]: We're still desiring them.

404
00:30:44,773 --> 00:30:45,314
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, that's the thing.

405
00:30:45,334 --> 00:30:48,617
[SPEAKER_05]: People, you start by saying no to things, and what do you do then?

406
00:30:48,637 --> 00:30:54,042
[SPEAKER_05]: Do you just hope now, that on account of your saying no, truth will become apparent?

407
00:30:54,402 --> 00:31:02,750
[SPEAKER_05]: Or, have you, once you said no, are you now reflecting and recollecting and cultivating insights into danger, peril, so that you don't have to forcefully say no?

408
00:31:03,591 --> 00:31:07,114
[SPEAKER_05]: You simply wouldn't want to go there, when you recognize the danger of it.

409
00:31:08,848 --> 00:31:15,552
[SPEAKER_03]: It's funny, because maybe some people say, you know, the pleasure of jhāna will help you not go back to sensuality.

410
00:31:15,572 --> 00:31:15,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

411
00:31:16,232 --> 00:31:17,193
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the common misconception.

412
00:31:17,213 --> 00:31:20,535
[SPEAKER_05]: The insight of danger of sensuality will not make you go back to sensuality.

413
00:31:20,555 --> 00:31:20,815
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

414
00:31:20,915 --> 00:31:21,756
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's the jhāna.

415
00:31:21,776 --> 00:31:23,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what the pleasure of it is.

416
00:31:24,097 --> 00:31:25,678
[SPEAKER_05]: Which is what the pleasure of jhāna is, yeah.

417
00:31:27,965 --> 00:31:31,628
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, this pleasure will surmount this joy and satisfaction.

418
00:31:31,688 --> 00:31:37,153
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, I'll get the pleasure so great, that I will not want any pleasure lesser than that.

419
00:31:37,353 --> 00:31:39,756
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, that's like, that's the definition of sensuality.

420
00:31:39,936 --> 00:31:43,879
[SPEAKER_05]: It's exactly like, let me just get this, and I'll never want anything else again.

421
00:31:45,340 --> 00:31:47,162
[SPEAKER_04]: That's like every sensual desire.

422
00:31:47,582 --> 00:31:49,364
[SPEAKER_04]: Doesn't matter how big or small it might be.

423
00:31:51,626 --> 00:31:53,668
[SPEAKER_03]: And even in that same sutta, it says,

424
00:31:55,772 --> 00:31:57,993
[SPEAKER_03]: He was thinking about second jhāna.

425
00:31:58,073 --> 00:31:58,953
[SPEAKER_03]: And he understood.

426
00:31:58,973 --> 00:32:03,735
[SPEAKER_03]: But why doesn't his mind launch out upon it and get there, yoniso?

427
00:32:03,775 --> 00:32:04,575
[SPEAKER_03]: The same thing applies.

428
00:32:04,615 --> 00:32:08,576
[SPEAKER_03]: It's because he was finding the light in that first jhāna, the thinking upon it.

429
00:32:09,196 --> 00:32:11,857
[SPEAKER_03]: And once he saw that as an affliction, the danger in that...

430
00:32:11,997 --> 00:32:14,838
[SPEAKER_05]: The danger, the obstacle, the lack of refinement, then, yeah.

431
00:32:15,538 --> 00:32:18,439
[SPEAKER_05]: When he withdrew himself from first jhāna into second.

432
00:32:19,039 --> 00:32:21,520
[SPEAKER_05]: So, first jhāna is the withdrawal from sensuality.

433
00:32:22,040 --> 00:32:23,981
[SPEAKER_05]: Second jhāna is withdrawal from the first jhāna.

434
00:32:24,401 --> 00:32:26,223
[SPEAKER_05]: Third jhāna is withdrawal from the second jhāna.

435
00:32:26,403 --> 00:32:28,105
[SPEAKER_05]: Fourth jhāna is withdrawal from the third jhāna.

436
00:32:28,885 --> 00:32:31,308
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not like, oh, I leap into it and then there's... No, no, no.

437
00:32:31,628 --> 00:32:44,641
[SPEAKER_05]: By completely withdrawing, abandoning desire for this, removing any, any kind of affinity towards it, seeing the danger, the peril, the lack of satisfaction, that's how you enter the next one.

438
00:32:46,234 --> 00:32:47,955
[SPEAKER_03]: I've seen the affliction of the first jhāna.

439
00:32:48,555 --> 00:32:48,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

440
00:32:49,075 --> 00:32:59,660
[SPEAKER_05]: And there's that sutta of the cow, the simile of the cow, where it steps on one stone and doesn't gain its balance and steadiness there, but it wants to step on the next one and then falls between the two.

441
00:32:59,680 --> 00:33:06,644
[SPEAKER_05]: It's the same, like, somebody starts withdrawing from sensuality, sends the first jhāna, but then he wants the second jhāna, and then he gets neither.

442
00:33:10,005 --> 00:33:13,427
[SPEAKER_05]: So, withdrawal from desires internally, externally, drying up.

